It's rather an issue of use semantics here --- Rserve can be used in  
parallel, JRI cannot. So you have to decide what paradigm you want to  
use and/or abstract it out as well. For example you can define that  
you are happy with serialized access, so you can abstract it out in  
start/stop methods that for JRI will keep the same session (start  
checks if JRI is running, if not, starts it and stop cleans up the  
global environment) whereas for Rserve start would simply create a  
connection and stop would close it.

In the same way that you can query REngine features we should probably  
add something like supportsMultipleInstances() and/or  
supportsParallelInstances() ... That would be cleaner than using  
instanceof conditions, but you are right that there is currently no  
other mechanism.


> In the meanwhile I will fix my code using the REngine factory method  
> for the engine initialization, as you suggest.
>

It's not a big deal, just that way you don't have to import JRIEngine  
and you get better checks against use of JRI-specific methods.

Cheers,
Simon



