On Jan 14, 2010, at 11:00 , Gabor Grothendieck wrote:

> I am using the H2 database (http://www.h2database.com) with RJDBC and
> if I use a create statement I can create a table with lower case
> column names but not if I use dbWriteTable.  The code below
> illustrates this.
>
> The problem is likely that the create statement that dbWriteTable is
> generating does not quote the column names as the explicit create
> statement shown below does.
>

I doesn't see any problem. Column names are case-insensitive by the  
original SQL standard so if the column names are valid identifies,  
there is not issues at all. To maximize compatibility RJDBC uses the  
lowest common denominator where possible and thus it doesn't use  
quoted identifiers (which are only supported by some databases and  
often not according to the standard) if possible. If your DBMS  
supports SQL properly there is no issue at all.

Cheers,
Simon



> First we show how to do it with an explicit create statement (it
> works) and then we try it with dbWriteTable (it does not work -- it
> creates upper case names rather than the case asked for).
>
