On Thu, Jan 14, 2010 at 2:11 PM, Simon Urbanek
<simon.urbanek at r-project.org> wrote:
>
> On Jan 14, 2010, at 11:00 , Gabor Grothendieck wrote:
>
>> I am using the H2 database (http://www.h2database.com) with RJDBC and
>> if I use a create statement I can create a table with lower case
>> column names but not if I use dbWriteTable.  The code below
>> illustrates this.
>>
>> The problem is likely that the create statement that dbWriteTable is
>> generating does not quote the column names as the explicit create
>> statement shown below does.
>>
>
> I doesn't see any problem. Column names are case-insensitive by the original
> SQL standard so if the column names are valid identifies, there is not
> issues at all. To maximize compatibility RJDBC uses the lowest common
> denominator where possible and thus it doesn't use quoted identifiers (which
> are only supported by some databases and often not according to the
> standard) if possible. If your DBMS supports SQL properly there is no issue
> at all.
>

Quoted identifiers are part of the SQL standard.  They are supported
by virtually every common database.  It is a problem because the way
RJDBC works now column names get converted to upper case.

In the standard quoted identifiers are called delimited identifiers.
Also note that there is an RJDBC  identifier.quote argument that could
be used to specify which one to be used (normally it would be " or '
).

RJDBC's dbWriteTable is generating this string (wrong):

    "CREATE TABLE BOD (Time DOUBLE PRECISION,demand DOUBLE PRECISION)"

whereas RSQLite's dbWriteTable (and presumably also RMySQL) generate
this string (correct for sqlite):

   "CREATE TABLE `BOD` \n( \"Time\" REAL,\n\tdemand REAL \n)"

where its the lack of quoting of the names in the first string that is
the problem.

In fact the way I found this problem was in trying to convert a short
test RSQLite based program to one based on RJDBC/H2 and expected it to
work out of the box since that is the whole idea of DBI and it almost
did work but the dbWriteTable problem prevented transparent
conversion.

From ggrothendieck at gmail.com  Wed Jan 20 22:39:29 2010
From: ggrothendieck at gmail.com (Gabor Grothendieck)
Date: Wed, 20 Jan 2010 17:39:29 -0500
Subject: passing parameters= to .jinit when using RJDBC
Message-ID: <971536df1001201439k5f9849bes723f6bcf1936fcc0@mail.gmail.com>

I am using a jar file that requires certain parameters to be set via
  java -Dparm=value
I would like to request that JDBC pass parameters to .jinit.
Currently the .jinit call in JDBC is .jinit(classPath) so there is no
way to pass parameters to it.

> RJDBC::JDBC
function (driverClass = "", classPath = "", identifier.quote = NA)
{
    .jinit(classPath)
    if (nchar(driverClass) &&
is.jnull(.jfindClass(as.character(driverClass)[1])))
        stop("Cannot find JDBC driver class ", driverClass)
    jdrv <- .jnew(driverClass, check = FALSE)
    .jcheck(TRUE)
    if (is.jnull(jdrv))
        jdrv <- .jnull()
    new("JDBCDriver", identifier.quote = as.character(identifier.quote),
        jdrv = jdrv)
}

