<simon.urbanek at r-project.org> wrote:
>
> On Jan 22, 2010, at 13:22 , Gabor Grothendieck wrote:
>
>> The thread is about the undesirability of global options and that this
>> problem is lessened if the option can be explicitly passed in an
>> argument so that you can be sure of the result without reliance on the
>> global parameters.
>>
>> Thus it would be better if we could write:
>>
>> myfn2 <- function(...args..., java.parameters = "-Dparam=value") {
>>  ...
>>  JDBC(...args..., java.parameters = java.parameters)
>>  ...
>> }
>>
>> Rather than
>>
>> myfn <- function(...,args..., java.parameters = "-Dparam=value") {
>>  ...
>>  options(java.parameters = java.parameters)
>>  JDBC(...args...)
>>  ...
>> }
>>
>> Note that this still leaves open the possibility to use global
>> parameters which can be convenient at times even if dangerous but it
>> also provides the ability to work without them.
>>
>
> The point is that both of the above are wrong. Such code is never guaranteed
> to work. java.parameters are global settings that you should setup in your
> environment. It is not a business of any function to set them - it's a user
> setting -- which is why it has nothing to do with the R-devel discussion.

It should not be the user's responsibility to set these.  Its up the
software to hide the details from the user to make the software easy
to use.  The user has no idea of what these parameters mean.  Although
I have had to code it globally due to the current at least I have been
able to hide it from the user but internally my code would have been a
bit nicer if the philosophy in the thread were followed.  In fact the
entire purpose of the software in question is to automatically handle
certain annoying details so that the user can work at a higher level.
No set up is required beyond ensuring Java is installed and the R
package is installed.

